.TITLE "rm"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Removes (deletes) files and directories on macOS. This command permanently
deletes specified files or directories from the filesystem. It is a powerful
utility for __filesystem management__, but caution is advised as deleted
items are not moved to the Trash and cannot be easily recovered.

.DIV "ALIAS"
**delete**

.DIV "TLDR"
**rm __file.txt__**
Removes a specific __file.txt__.
> __file.txt__: The path to the file to remove.

**rm -i __file.txt__**
Removes a __file.txt__ with an interactive prompt before deletion.
> __-i__: interactive flag | __file.txt__: The path to the file to remove.

**rm -f __file.txt__**
Removes a __file.txt__ forcefully, without prompting for confirmation.
> __-f__: force flag | __file.txt__: The path to the file to remove.

**rm -r __directory__**
Recursively removes a __directory__ and all its contents.
> __-r__: recursive flag | __directory__: The path to the directory to remove.

**rm -rf __directory__**
Recursively removes a __directory__ and its contents forcefully.
> __-r__: recursive flag | __-f__: force flag | __directory__: The path to the directory to remove.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
The `rm -rf` command is extremely dangerous and should be used with extreme
caution, especially with root privileges, as it can quickly delete critical
system files. Always double-check your path before executing `rm -rf`.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**mac:mv** **mac:cp**

documentation: https://ss64.com/osx/rm.html
